BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:tech-policy-press-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T212730Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260924
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260925
SUMMARY:Deadline: Tech Policy Press Fellowship Program 2027
DESCRIPTION:Tech Policy Press is recruiting its 2027 fellowship cohort to p
 ursue independent reporting and analysis on urgent questions at the inters
 ection of technology and democracy. Since 2023 the programme has supported
  25 fellows from 11 countries across three cohorts\, writing on AI governa
 nce\, tech regulation\, digital rights and the entanglement of state and c
 orporate power. WHO IT IS FOR: mid-career journalists\, researchers\, lawy
 ers and public policy professionals with a strong background in a specific
  tech policy area\, a particular geography\, or technology's impact on dem
 ocracy and society. Applicants from the Global Majority and from communiti
 es underrepresented in media are encouraged\, as are projects that centre 
 communities and regions usually excluded from global tech policy conversat
 ions. Contributions can range from original reporting to policy analysis\,
  and technical practitioners are welcome as long as the work fits the publ
 ication's editorial purview. EXPECTATIONS: at least one piece of analysis 
 or reporting per month (exclusive to Tech Policy Press\, though it may be 
 republished elsewhere)\, a mandatory hour-long monthly cohort meeting\, a 
 monthly check-in with the editorial team\, collaboration with other fellow
 s and the masthead\, agreed deadlines\, and disclosure of conflicts of int
 erest. Applicants should arrive with a clear area of focus and a sense of 
 how the work will inform both the public and decision-makers. APPLY via th
 e online form by 11:59pm EDT on 24 September 2026\, with a CV or resume\, 
 a cover letter of no more than two pages\, links to prior publications (op
 en access\, published within the past three years\, at least one in Englis
 h and at least one solo-authored\; social posts\, pre-prints and unpublish
 ed coursework do not count) and two professional references. Shortlisted c
 andidates may be invited to an online interview. Decisions are expected by
  early December 2026. Two optional Zoom information sessions run on 9 Sept
 ember at 8am EDT and 16 September at 7pm EDT\; registration required.\n\nO
 rganisation: Tech Policy Press\nLocation: Fully remote\, worldwide. Intern
 ational applicants explicitly encouraged\; fellows work as independent con
 tractors. No requirement to be authorised to work in the USA.\nAward: USD 
 10\,000 stipend per fellow\, paid quarterly. Also included: editorial guid
 ance from the Tech Policy Press team\, membership of an international coho
 rt with regular feedback and exchange\, and monthly sessions to strengthen
  the work. Fellows' work is published on the Tech Policy Press website and
  promoted through the newsletter\, podcast and events. No application fee.
 \nDuration: Twelve months\, part-time\, running January to December 2027\n
 \nMore: https://www.techpolicy.press/call-for-applications-2027-tech-polic
 y-press-fellowship-program/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medi
 um=referral
URL:https://www.techpolicy.press/call-for-applications-2027-tech-policy-pre
 ss-fellowship-program/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=re
 ferral
LOCATION:Fully remote\, worldwide. International applicants explicitly enco
 uraged\; fellows work as independent contractors. No requirement to be aut
 horised to work in the USA.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:sundance-episodic-program-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T212730Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260825
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260826
SUMMARY:Deadline: Sundance Institute: Episodic Program and Lab 2027
DESCRIPTION:The Sundance Institute Episodic Program is an entry point for e
 merging to mid-career writers and creators working across all scripted ser
 ies platforms\, combining a lab experience with customised support to move
  projects and careers forward. The Episodic Lab lets writers workshop an o
 riginal pilot and develop their writing and pitching skills: working with 
 showrunners\, producers and executives\, fellows take part in one-on-one s
 tory meetings\, pitching sessions\, craft workshops and writers rooms. Up 
 to ten applicants are selected\, either for the in-person Lab (preceded by
  virtual workshops) or for a fully virtual Intensive with a creative advis
 or. ELIGIBILITY: applicants must be 18 or older\; the script must be the a
 pplicant's original creation or adapted from optioned source material and 
 written in English\; the draft must be revised or new\, not already produc
 ed\, not sold to a third party and not distributed online\; and applicants
  cannot have previously had an episodic project (pilot or pitch) produced 
 by a studio or network. Open to international and U.S. participants\, subj
 ect to travel guidelines. Up to two writers per project may participate\, 
 with one named as primary applicant. APPLICATION: submitted via FilmFreewa
 y\, including writer bios (250 words each)\, a series logline (50 words)\,
  pilot synopsis (500 words)\, one-page series overview (500 words)\, perso
 nal statement (250 words)\, community connection\, thematic statement (250
  words)\, characters and core relationships (250 words)\, episodic vision\
 , optional video samples (up to three links\, 10 minutes total)\, and the 
 full pilot script as a PDF in standard screenplay format. Demographic ques
 tions are optional and do not affect consideration. Candidates may submit 
 up to three applications\, with a separate application and USD 50 fee for 
 each project. Submissions opened 13 July 2026 and close 25 August 2026 at 
 6:00 p.m. PT. Finalists are notified by email through FilmFreeway around m
 id to late March 2027 (FilmFreeway lists 31 March 2027)\, and the list of 
 selected writers and projects is published at sundance.org/epi after 15 Ap
 ril 2027. Finalists must return a Fellow Agreement\, a Participation Agree
 ment and any other required documents within five days. Questions: episodi
 cprogram@sundance.org.\n\nOrganisation: Sundance Institute\nLocation: Open
  to international and U.S. applicants. In-person Lab takes place in the At
 lanta\, Georgia area\, USA in Spring 2027\; the Intensive track is fully o
 nline.\nAward: No stipend or honorarium for participation. For the in-pers
 on Lab\, Sundance covers round-trip basic economy airfare from the airport
  nearest the fellow's residence\, shared housing with a private room and b
 ath\, and ground transport to and from the airport (fellows within 100 mil
 es of the Lab get ground transport only). Meals\, in-room charges\, travel
  insurance\, luggage fees and taxes are the fellow's own cost. One selecte
 d project also receives a separate monetary grant from the Alfred P. Sloan
  Foundation for a series with an illuminating emphasis on science\, includ
 ing medicine\, energy\, technology or invention. APPLICATION FEE: USD 50 n
 onrefundable processing fee per project submitted.\nDuration: In-person La
 b in Spring 2027 (event dates 15 to 20 April 2027) preceded by virtual wor
 kshops\; the Intensive track convenes virtually in Summer 2027.\n\nMore: h
 ttps://filmfreeway.com/SundanceEpisodicLab2027?utm_source=artificialnouvea
 ugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://filmfreeway.com/SundanceEpisodicLab2027?utm_source=artificialno
 uveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Open to international and U.S. applicants. In-person Lab takes pla
 ce in the Atlanta\, Georgia area\, USA in Spring 2027\; the Intensive trac
 k is fully online.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:beinecke-short-term-fellowships-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T212730Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260815
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260816
SUMMARY:Deadline: Beinecke Library Short-Term Research Fellowships 2027-202
 8 (Yale)
DESCRIPTION:The Beinecke Library Fellowship Program offers competitive shor
 t-term fellowships to support research projects that substantively engage 
 with Yale Library Special Collections materials. WHO CAN APPLY: academic a
 nd independent scholars\, locally and globally\, with or without an instit
 utional affiliation\; the library explicitly welcomes applicants regardles
 s of institutional association\, race\, cultural background\, ability\, se
 xual orientation\, gender or socioeconomic status. Applications using trad
 itional archival and bibliographic research methods are encouraged\, and s
 o are creative\, interdisciplinary and non-traditional approaches to worki
 ng with the collections\, which makes this a live option for artists and w
 riters whose practice is research-driven. WHAT YOU GET: USD 5\,000 per mon
 th for an onsite research visit of between two weeks and two months\, paid
  at the start of the fellowship whenever possible (international fellows s
 hould expect payment to take several weeks)\; fellows pay their own taxes 
 on the award. TIMELINE: applications for the 2027-2028 awards opened 1 Jun
 e 2026 and close 15 August 2026\, via Interfolio\; all decisions are notif
 ied by 31 January 2027\; research must be conducted between 1 July 2027 an
 d 30 June 2028\, and awards cannot be deferred.\n\nOrganisation: Beinecke 
 Rare Book & Manuscript Library\, Yale University\nLocation: Onsite at Yale
  Library Special Collections\, New Haven\, Connecticut\, USA. Open to acad
 emic and independent scholars locally and globally\, regardless of institu
 tional association.\nAward: USD 5\,000 per month for research visits betwe
 en two weeks and two months. Funding is paid at the beginning of the fello
 wship where possible (payment to international fellows often takes several
  weeks)\; fellows are responsible for taxes on the award. Free to apply.\n
 Duration: Two weeks to two months of onsite research\, conducted between 1
  July 2027 and 30 June 2028\; awards cannot be deferred\n\nMore: https://b
 einecke.library.yale.edu/beinecke/researchers/fellowships/short-term?utm_s
 ource=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://beinecke.library.yale.edu/beinecke/researchers/fellowships/shor
 t-term?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Onsite at Yale Library Special Collections\, New Haven\, Connectic
 ut\, USA. Open to academic and independent scholars locally and globally\,
  regardless of institutional association.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:pulitzer-center-ai-accountability-fellowships-2026-2027@artificialnouve
 au.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T212730Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260712
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260713
SUMMARY:Deadline: Pulitzer Center: AI Accountability Fellowships 2026-2027
DESCRIPTION:The AI Accountability Fellowships support journalists working o
 n in-depth AI accountability stories that examine how governments and corp
 orations use predictive\, generative and surveillance technologies to guid
 e decisions in policing\, medicine\, social welfare\, criminal justice\, h
 iring and more. Designed for reporters from all beats\, desks and formats\
 , the fellowship asks applicants to propose a concrete reporting project (
 with evidence of pre-reporting) that uses approaches such as data analysis
 \, records requests and shoe-leather reporting to examine the real-world i
 mpact of algorithms. For the first time this year\, the fellowship also in
 cludes funding\, mentorship and training to develop and execute an impact/
 engagement plan to reach strategic audiences. SUPPORT: up to USD 25\,000 p
 er fellow (up to USD 20\,000 reporting plus USD 5\,000 engagement)\, paid 
 in three installments\; freelancers may budget up to one third as a stipen
 d\, while newsrooms cover staff salaries. Fellows also receive mentors\, s
 pecialized training\, pro bono legal and public records support\, and a la
 sting community of peers. ELIGIBILITY: staff or freelance journalists acro
 ss print\, radio\, video and multimedia\, able to work collaboratively\; r
 eporters may be based anywhere (fellowship is remote). Experience reportin
 g on AI is not required\, but a track record of in-depth\, impactful repor
 ting and investigative/data/explanatory experience is valued. Small teams 
 may apply with a designated lead Fellow. REQUIREMENTS: a mandatory monthly
  1.5-2 hour meeting\, at least one community call\, engagement with other 
 fellows\, and sharing of methodologies and lessons learned\; fellowship co
 mmunication\, meetings and training are in English\, but fellows may publi
 sh in any language. APPLY: submit a 500-word statement of purpose\, a 500-
 word project description with reporting plan\, an engagement plan\, a budg
 et\, three links to recent work\, a letter of commitment from a publishing
  media organization (or editor support letter for staff)\, three reference
 s and a CV. Funded with support from the MacArthur Foundation\, Luminate\,
  Omidyar Network and others. Contact: reacheditorial@pulitzercenter.org. D
 eadline: 12 July 2026\, 11:59pm EDT.\n\nOrganisation: Pulitzer Center\nLoc
 ation: Worldwide\; reporters may be based anywhere and the fellowship is r
 emote. Proposals from the Global South and from journalists representing a
  broad array of backgrounds are encouraged.\nAward: Up to USD 25\,000 per 
 fellow\, paid in three installments (up to USD 20\,000 for reporting and U
 SD 5\,000 for engagement activities)\; covers records requests\, travel\, 
 data analysis and engagement costs. Freelancers may budget up to one third
  of the total as a stipend\; newsrooms are expected to pay staff salaries.
  No application fee.\nDuration: 10 months\, expected September 2026 to Jul
 y 2027\n\nMore: https://pulitzercenter.org/grants-fellowships/opportunitie
 s-journalists/ai-accountability-fellowships?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://pulitzercenter.org/grants-fellowships/opportunities-journalists
 /ai-accountability-fellowships?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_m
 edium=referral
LOCATION:Worldwide\; reporters may be based anywhere and the fellowship is 
 remote. Proposals from the Global South and from journalists representing 
 a broad array of backgrounds are encouraged.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lawai-academic-fellowship-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T212730Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260731
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260801
SUMMARY:Deadline: Law & AI Academic Fellowship
DESCRIPTION:The Institute for Law & AI (LawAI)\, an independent think tank 
 researching and advising on the legal challenges posed by artificial intel
 ligence\, is running its inaugural Law & AI Academic Fellowship for aspiri
 ng legal scholars who intend to go on the US legal academic job market. Th
 e fellowship is designed as an alternative to university-sponsored Visitin
 g Assistant Professor or law fellow positions: fellows spend the majority 
 of their time researching and writing articles for publication in law jour
 nals on the law of transformative AI\, and are prepared for the academic j
 ob market as part of a larger application package. Teaching is not require
 d\, which LawAI frames as a benefit over most university-affiliated positi
 ons\, though fellows who want teaching experience are encouraged to seek i
 t and LawAI and its affiliates may be able to help secure positions. Each 
 fellow is paired with an academic mentor from LawAI's network of affiliate
 d academics for ongoing support\, with more intensive help in the final st
 ages of the job application process including moot interviews and detailed
  feedback. Fellows may also inquire about spending time in residence at ac
 ademic institutions of interest. Candidates are selected in part on how we
 ll their research interests align with LawAI's: the institute expects AI t
 o reshape culture\, the economy\, public policy\, public administration\, 
 politics\, military affairs and geostrategy at least as profoundly as the 
 Industrial Revolution\, and asks whether a candidate's research is well-sc
 oped to changes of that magnitude. Areas of particular interest include ri
 sk regulation of transformative AI\, liability for AI harms\, AI and biose
 curity\, AI and cybersecurity\, executive branch use of transformative AI\
 , national security law\, law and AI infrastructure\, administrative law\,
  constitutional law\, international regulation\, AI in the judiciary\, and
  industrial policy. Full-time two-year role at USD 130\,000 per year\, bas
 ed in Washington DC\, Cambridge UK\, or remote. Applications close 31 July
  2026.\n\nOrganisation: Institute for Law & AI (LawAI)\nLocation: Washingt
 on DC\, USA\; Cambridge\, UK\; or remote\nAward: USD 130\,000 annual salar
 y. No application fee.\nDuration: Two years\, full-time\n\nMore: https://l
 aw-ai.org/career/academic-fellowship/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdes
 k&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://law-ai.org/career/academic-fellowship/?utm_source=artificialnou
 veaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Washington DC\, USA\; Cambridge\, UK\; or remote
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:kennan-institute-digital-fellowship-2026-2027@artificialnouveau.github.
 io
DTSTAMP:20260901T212730Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260814
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260815
SUMMARY:Deadline: Kennan Institute Digital Fellowship 2026-2027
DESCRIPTION:The inaugural Kennan Institute Digital Fellowship promotes orig
 inal scholarship and quality non-fiction writing about Russia and the form
 er Soviet Union\, with an emphasis on work that has policy relevance. Ten 
 fellowships are awarded per year\, and AT LEAST FOUR POSITIONS ARE RESERVE
 D FOR RESEARCHERS PURSUING TOPICS IN THE NATURAL SCIENCES\, MEDICINE\, TEC
 HNOLOGY OR RELATED FIELDS\, which is the route by which a technology-focus
 ed project fits this call. The programme aims to give participants time\, 
 professional resources and support while bringing their work to a wider pu
 blic and to the Washington-based policy community\, and to build a cohort 
 network that continues into the alumni body. Each fellow receives a monthl
 y stipend of USD 2\,000\, mentorship and consultation from the Kennan comm
 unity of experts where applicable\, six virtual webinars tailored to the c
 ohort's needs\, and membership of the alumni network. ELIGIBILITY: intende
 d for individuals with several years of experience working on\, studying o
 r otherwise examining Russia and/or broader Eurasia\; applicants must have
  a good working knowledge of English\, as the fellowship is conducted only
  in English and any publications produced for the Kennan Institute appear 
 first in English with possible later translation into regional languages. 
 The programme runs entirely virtually from 1 October 2026 to 31 March 2027
 \; the Institute does not cover travel-related expenses including visas. S
 upported by the Lounsbery Foundation and the U.S. Russia Foundation. Deadl
 ine 14 August 2026\, 17:00 EST. NOTE: 'digital' here describes the virtual
  format\, not the subject matter\, so this fits the desk only if your proj
 ect is itself about technology\, data or science.\n\nOrganisation: Kennan 
 Institute\, Woodrow Wilson International Center for Scholars (supported by
  the Lounsbery Foundation and the U.S. Russia Foundation)\nLocation: Fully
  virtual. No travel is funded and no travel is required.\nAward: USD 2\,00
 0 monthly stipend for the six-month programme\, plus mentorship from the K
 ennan community of experts\, six virtual webinars tailored to the cohort\,
  and inclusion in the Kennan alumni network of over 4\,500 people. Travel 
 and visa costs are explicitly not covered. No application fee.\nDuration: 
 Six months\, 1 October 2026 to 31 March 2027\, followed by several months 
 of possible integration into the Kennan Institute's event schedule\n\nMore
 : https://www.kennaninstitute.org/digital-fellowship?utm_source=artificial
 nouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.kennaninstitute.org/digital-fellowship?utm_source=artificia
 lnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Fully virtual. No travel is funded and no travel is required.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:bulletin-editorial-fellowship-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T212730Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260828
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260829
SUMMARY:Deadline: Bulletin of the Atomic Scientists Editorial Fellowship 20
 26
DESCRIPTION:The Bulletin of the Atomic Scientists runs an Editorial Fellows
  Program for emerging leaders who can give its audience regular and expans
 ive coverage of the Bulletin's core issues\, the existential threats facin
 g humanity. TRACKS: two specialty tracks are open in this round\, biosecur
 ity and disruptive technology/AI. Fellows write for the Bulletin's editori
 al pages\, producing up to four published pieces over the year at USD 750 
 each\, with limited travel costs and occasional incidental expenses covere
 d on approval. WHO SHOULD APPLY: this is not a student programme. Applican
 ts need significant professional experience\, an established record of pub
 lishing quality work\, and recognition as an emerging leader in their fiel
 d\; undergraduate and graduate students may apply only if they bring subst
 antial writing experience and published work. No geographic or citizenship
  restriction is stated. HOW TO APPLY: email a resume\, a cover letter (the
  call stresses that the cover letter matters)\, and up to three writing sa
 mples\, with 'editorial fellow' as the subject line\, to the address given
  on the call page. Fellows are announced in September 2026. Deadline 28 Au
 gust 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Bulletin of the Atomic Scientists\nLocation: R
 emote. No geographic or citizenship restriction is stated.\nAward: USD 750
  per published piece\, for up to four pieces annually\, so up to USD 3\,00
 0. Limited travel costs and occasional incidental expenses are also covere
 d. No application fee.\nDuration: Annual fellowship\, built around up to f
 our published pieces\n\nMore: https://thebulletin.org/2026/08/apply-to-be-
 a-2026-bulletin-editorial-fellow/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&ut
 m_medium=referral
URL:https://thebulletin.org/2026/08/apply-to-be-a-2026-bulletin-editorial-f
 ellow/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote. No geographic or citizenship restriction is stated.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:adina-talve-goodman-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T212730Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261013
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261014
SUMMARY:Deadline: One Story: Adina Talve-Goodman Fellowship 2027
DESCRIPTION:Annual educational fellowship honouring the memory of author an
 d former One Story managing editor Adina Talve-Goodman: a year-long mentor
 ship on the craft of fiction writing with One Story magazine\, aimed at gi
 ving a writer outside the fold a significant career boost. WHAT THE FELLOW
  RECEIVES: access to One Story online classes\; admission to the Writing C
 ircle\; a USD 2\,000 stipend and free admission to the week-long summer wr
 iters' conference with craft lectures\, an intensive fiction workshop and 
 panels with literary agents and publishers\; and a full manuscript review 
 and consultation with executive editor Hannah Tinti (story collection or n
 ovel in progress up to 150 pages / 35\,000 words). ELIGIBILITY: early-care
 er fiction writers who have not yet published a book\, have never been enr
 olled in an advanced degree programme (MA or MFA) in creative writing\, En
 glish or literature and have no plans to attend one in 2027\, and are at l
 east 21 years old as of 1 January 2027. Applicants outside the United Stat
 es are welcome. The fellowship seeks writing that centres\, celebrates or 
 reclaims being marginalised through the lens of race\, ethnicity\, gender\
 , sexuality\, age\, class\, religion\, illness\, disability\, trauma\, mig
 ration\, displacement\, dispossession or imprisonment. APPLY via Submittab
 le with a fiction sample of 3\,000 to 5\,000 words (multiple stories allow
 ed within the limit\, one file)\, a personal statement of 400 to 750 words
 \, a statement of purpose of 200 to 350 words\, two references (contact de
 tails only\, no letters) and a current resume including writing classes\, 
 awards\, publications and residencies. Applications open 8 September and c
 lose 13 October 2026 at 23:59 ET\; the winner is announced in January 2027
 .\n\nOrganisation: One Story (with the Talve-Goodman Family)\nLocation: Re
 mote year-long mentorship with One Story magazine (Brooklyn\, US)\; includ
 es free admission to the week-long summer writers' conference. Open to app
 licants outside the United States.\nAward: USD 2\,000 stipend plus free ad
 mission to One Story's week-long summer writers' conference\, access to On
 e Story online classes\, admission to the Writing Circle\, and a full manu
 script review and consultation with Executive Editor Hannah Tinti (story c
 ollection or novel in progress up to 150 pages / 35\,000 words). No applic
 ation fee.\nDuration: Year-long fellowship for 2027. Applications open 8 S
 eptember 2026 and close 13 October 2026 at 23:59 ET\; winner announced pub
 licly in January 2027.\n\nMore: https://one-story.com/learn/fellowship/?ut
 m_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://one-story.com/learn/fellowship/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugra
 ntdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote year-long mentorship with One Story magazine (Brooklyn\, US
 )\; includes free admission to the week-long summer writers' conference. O
 pen to applicants outside the United States.
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
